 "spot": [
  {
   "i": 152,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "We have alluded already to the fact that the simple typed theory of sets TST can be shown to be equivalent to an untyped theory (Mac Lane set theory, aka bounded Zermelo set theory). We briefly indicate how to do this: choose any map ⟦M⟧ in the model which is an injection with domain the set of singletons of type 0 objects and range included in type 1 (the identity on singletons of type 0 objects is an example). Identify each type 0 object ⟦M⟧ with the type 1 object ⟦M⟧ ; then introduce exactly ",
   "md_text": "We have alluded already to the fact that the simple typed theory of sets TST can be shown to be equivalent to an untyped theory (Mac Lane set theory, aka bounded Zermelo set theory). We briefly indicate how to do this: choose any map ⟦M⟧ in the model which is an injection with domain the set of singletons of type 0 objects and range included in type 1 (the identity on singletons of type 0 objects is an example). Identify each type 0 object ⟦M⟧ with the type 1 object ⟦M⟧ ; then introduce exactly "
  },
  {
   "i": 9,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "Dedekind proposed that the real numbers could be uniquely correlated with cuts in the rationals, where a cut was determined by a pair of sets ⟦M⟧ with the following properties: ⟦M⟧ and ⟦M⟧ are sets of rationals. ⟦M⟧ and ⟦M⟧ are both nonempty and every element of ⟦M⟧ is less than every element of ⟦M⟧ (so the two sets are disjoint). ⟦M⟧ has no greatest element. The union of ⟦M⟧ and ⟦M⟧ contains all rationals.",
   "md_text": "Dedekind proposed that the real numbers could be uniquely correlated with cuts in the rationals, where a cut was determined by a pair of sets ⟦M⟧ with the following properties: ⟦M⟧ and ⟦M⟧ are sets of rationals. ⟦M⟧ and ⟦M⟧ are both nonempty and every element of ⟦M⟧ is less than every element of ⟦M⟧ (so the two sets are disjoint). ⟦M⟧ has no greatest element. The union of ⟦M⟧ and ⟦M⟧ contains all rationals."
  }
